      "text": "To find the inverse of a composition of functions, we can use the property that $(f \\circ g) \\circ h = g \\circ h \\circ f$. In other words, to find the inverse of a composition of functions, we must apply the inverse of the outermost function first, then apply the inverse of the next function, and so on.\n\nIn this case, we have $f = q \\circ p \\circ r$. To find the inverse of $f$, we must apply the inverse of $r$ first, then the inverse of $p$, and then the inverse of $q$. Therefore, the correct expression for $f^{-1}$ is $r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}$.\n\nWe can verify this by substituting $f^{-1}$ into the original equation and checking that it satisfies the equation $f^{-1} \\circ f = I$, where $I$ is the identity function.\n\nLet's check:\n\n1. First, we apply $f^{-1}$ to both sides: $(f^{-1} \\circ f) \\circ g = (I \\circ g)$\n2. Next, we apply $f$ to both sides: $l = q \\circ p \\circ r$\n3. Finally, we apply $f^{-1}$ to both sides again: $I = f^{-1} \\circ (q \\circ p \\circ r) = f^{-1} \\circ q \\circ I \\circ p \\circ r = f^{-1} \\circ q \\circ p^{-1} \\circ r^{-1}$\n\nThis shows that the expression $r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}$ correctly represents the inverse of $f$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}$, so the letter we choose is \\boxed{C}.",
