      "text": "$f^{-1} = r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}.$\n\nLet's verify the solution by working through the expressions algebraically and then applying Python code for simulation. We will use specific function mappings for each of the four functions to ensure our solution is correct.\n\n### Step-by-step solution:\n\n1. Define the original functions:\n    \\[\n    p(x), \\quad q(x), \\quad r(x)\n    \\]\n\n2. Define the composite function: \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\).\n\n3. Find the inverse function for the entire composite function \\( f \\), denoted \\( f^{-1} \\).\n\nLet's take a look at the provided solutions:\n- For option A: \\( r^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\)\n- For option B: \\( p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ r^{-1} \\)\n- For option C: \\( r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\)\n- For option D: \\( q^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ r^{-1} \\)\n- For option E: \\( q^{-1} \\circ r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\)\n\nWe will use specific mappings to simulate these composite functions and invert their order. We will assume the following:\n-\\( p(x) = x + 1 \\)\n- \\( q(x) = \\frac{x}{2} \\)\n- \\( r(x) = 2x \\)\n\n### Mapping definitions:\n\n- \\( p(x) = x + 1 \\)\n- \\( q(x) = \\frac{x}{2} \\)\n- \\( r(x) = 2x \\)\n\n### Step 1: Compositional function \\( f \\) computation\n\\[\n f(x) = q(p(r(x))) = q(p(2x)) = q(2x+1) = \\frac{2x+1}{2}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Inverted function formation\n\nTo determine the correct relationship for the inverse of \\( f \\), we need to compute the composite of the inverse functions in reverse order:\n\\[\n f^{-1} = r^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ p^{-1}\n\\]\n\nWe will first derive the inverses for each of these transformations. Given specific function definitions, we compute:\n1. \\( p^{-1}(x) \\)\n2. \\( q^{-1}(x) \\)\n3. \\( r^{-1}(x) \\)\n\nWe will calculate these inverses explicitly and then verify the product of the inverses to determine \\( f^{-1} \\).\n```python\n# Define the original functions\ndef p(x):\n    return x + 1\n\ndef q