      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the inverse of a composition of functions, we reverse each composition. In other words, the inverse of a composition is the inverse of the function first, then the inverse of the function next, and so on.\n\nWe are given $f = q \\circ p \\circ r.$ Let's look at the chain of inverse functions. First, we find the inverse of $f.$ Since $f$ is a composition of function $q \\circ p \\circ r,$ we first find the inverse of each function:\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf^{-1} &= (q \\circ r \\circ p)^{-1} \\\\\n&\\text{Substituting the inverse of each function:}\\\\\n&= (p \\circ r \\circ q)^{-1}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo, we have $f^{-1} = p \\circ r \\circ q.$ Now, we need to match this with one of the options.\n\nLet's expand option B: $p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ r^{-1}.$\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ r^{-1}(x) &= r^{-1}(q^{-1}(p^{-1}(x))) \\\\\n&= r^{-1}(q^{-1}(q \\circ r(x))) \\ \\ = \\ \\ r^{-1}(r(x)) \\ \\= \\ x.\n\\end{align*}\n\nSince $p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1} \\circ r^{-1}(x) = x,$ option B is correct!\n\nThe answer is $\\boxed{\\text{B}}.$",
